#--: Overview

The declarations live in #~"lib/system/zd.z", which is the one place they are
written; every definition there carries its own doc comment, and #~"zls" shows
each on hover. This page explains how the unit is used.

The #~"zd" unit reads and writes [#> to=./zdata.html: data files] (#~".zd")
while a program runs. A read turns the file into a tree of #b:"entries". Every
value sits in an #~"Entry", which says where the value was written, under which
name, at which path, and with which type, and holds a #~"Node": the value
itself. The node is a union, so a #~"match" on it must name every arm.

A read needs no compiler in the program. It checks each field and arm name
against the file's own #~"data" and #~"choice" declarations, and it applies the
defaults those declarations give. A file that needs the compiler -- one that
names another unit's type, refers to another definition, or computes a value
with an operator or an interpolation -- is refused, and the refusal names what
and where. #~"zc data" stays the full check of a data file.

#--: Reading

#~"zd.read path:" reads a file; #~"zd.parse source: file:" reads text that a
program already holds. Both answer a #~"Result": the file's entry, or a
#~"zd.Error". An error's text is #~"file:line:column: path: what", and
#~"orPanic" prints it when the program cannot go on.

A file #~"known.zd" that declares #~"failure: data { program: text  stage:
text }" and lists its values in #~"failures: list { ... }" reads so:

#code language=zerolang: """
  main: function is {
      doc: (zd.read path: "known.zd").orPanic
      for each row: (doc.itemsAt path: "failures").orPanic loop {
          program: (row.textAt path: "program").orPanic
          stage: (row.textAt path: "stage").orPanic
          print "\{program} (\{stage})"
      }
  }
  """

#--: The tree

A #~"Node" is one of eight arms:

#ul:
  [#*: #~"integer" -- a number with no fraction: #~"5", #~"-3", #~"0xff",
    #~"8080.u16". It is read as an #~"i64"; a value outside it is refused.]
  [#*: #~"float" -- a number with a fraction or an exponent: #~"2.5". An
    integer written where a float type is declared is read as that float.]
  [#*: #~"text" -- a string, its escapes decoded.]
  [#*: #~"boolean" -- #~"true" or #~"false".]
  [#*: #~"listValue" -- #~"list { ... }": the items in order. A labelled
    item's label is its entry's name.]
  [#*: #~"dataValue" -- #~"(T f: v ...)": every field of #~"T" in the order
    its declaration gives them. A field the value leaves out holds its
    default; one with no default must be written.]
  [#*: #~"choiceValue" -- #~"T.arm", or #~"T.arm (payload)": the arm, and
    the entry it carries (none for an arm declared #~"null").]
  [#*: #~"unitValue" -- #~"unit { ... }", and the file itself: its value
    definitions in order. A file's #~"data" and #~"choice" declarations are
    no values and are not in the tree.]

An #~"Entry" holds #~"file", #~"name" (the definition, field or label; empty
for an unlabelled item), #~"at" (line and column), #~"path" and #~"type" (the
type written: #~"u16", #~"failure", #~"stage", a list's #~"out" type; empty
when none was written) besides its #~"node".

#--: Paths and accessors

A #b:"path" names an entry below another: a field of a data value or a
definition of a unit by name, an item of a list by #~"[n]" or by its label, and
a step into what a choice value carries. Steps are joined by #~".":
#~"failures[1].stage.count". The empty path names the entry itself.

Each accessor reads one kind of value at a path, and answers its error when the
path names nothing or another kind of value:

#ul:
  [#*: #~"entryAt", #~"itemsAt", #~"textAt", #~"armAt", #~"payloadAt" -- a
    #~"ResultView": a readonly view into the tree, which stays owned by the
    entry it was read from.]
  [#*: #~"integerAt", #~"floatAt", #~"booleanAt" -- a #~"ResultVR" holding
    the value.]

#--: Writing

#~"zd.write path: header: schema: doc:" writes the file's unit #~"doc" after
the comment block #~"header" and the declarations #~"schema", both written as
given: a program that writes its own data file knows its types, and keeps them
as text. #~"zd.format" answers the same text without writing it. The values
are written in the form #~"zl fmt" keeps, every field of a data value
included, so what one program writes another reads back to the same entries.
